course: technology
lesson: 28
title: Diggers, cranes and cement mixers
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Building machines are giants with one big trick each. A digger scoops, a crane lifts, a mixer lorry keeps concrete moving, a roller squashes, and a pile driver hammers posts deep into the ground.
explain: Watch a digger at the roadworks: the driver nudges small levers, and the huge arm lifts a bucket of earth heavier than a car. The engine drives a pump that pushes oil into strong tubes called pistons, and oil cannot be squashed, so it pushes the arm with enormous force. This is called hydraulics.
explain: A tower crane works like a seesaw. The long arm carries the load on a trolley and cable, and heavy concrete blocks on the short arm balance it, so the crane does not tip over.
explain: All these machines are amazing to watch, and the only safe place to watch them from is behind the fence. Building sites are dangerous even when they look empty, so children never go in.
fact: A digger's engine drives a pump that pushes oil into pistons, called hydraulics, which move the arm and bucket with huge force; its tracks spread its weight so it does not sink in mud.
fact: Diggers swing round and their drivers cannot see behind them; never go onto a building site, and keep behind the fences.
fact: A tower crane has a tall mast and a long arm with a trolley and cable for the load, and heavy concrete weights on the short arm balance it; it grows taller by adding pieces to the mast as the building rises.
fact: A tower crane driver usually climbs a long ladder inside the mast and works high up in the cab for hours; cranes stop in high winds, and nobody stands under a load.
fact: A mobile crane is a crane on a lorry with an arm that slides out like a telescope; it puts out legs before lifting so it does not tip over.
fact: A cement mixer lorry keeps its drum turning so the concrete does not set; spiral fins inside mix it one way and push it out the other way.
fact: Concrete sets because of a chemical reaction with water, not because it dries; wet cement can burn skin.
fact: A bulldozer pushes earth and rubble with a big blade at the front, and its caterpillar tracks grip the ground; tracks were used on farm tractors before tanks.
fact: A dumper truck has a big bin that tips up to empty; the largest mining dump trucks carry hundreds of tonnes and have tyres taller than an adult.
fact: A road roller squashes soil and hot asphalt (the black stuff that roads are covered with) flat with heavy steel drums, and some shake thousands of times a minute to pack the ground better.
fact: A pile driver drives long posts, called piles, deep into the ground to hold up buildings by carrying their weight down to firm ground, and tall buildings can stand on piles deeper than a house is tall; it is so loud that workers wear ear protection.
fact: A cherry picker lifts workers on a platform at the end of a folding hydraulic arm, and they wear harnesses clipped to the platform; fire brigades use similar machines to reach high windows, and council workers use them to fix street lights.
fact: Hard hats protect heads from falling things, and hi-vis vests make workers easy to see; hi-vis means high visibility.
fact: Many dumper trucks used on building sites have 6 wheels.
exercise: How can a small push on a lever lift a whole bucket of earth? | The engine's pump pushes oil into pistons with huge force | The driver's arm is connected to the bucket by a rope | The bucket is filled with air to make it light | The digger uses magnets to pull up the earth
exercise: Why does a digger have tracks instead of wheels? | Tracks spread its weight so it does not sink in mud | Tracks are much cheaper than big rubber tyres and never wear out | Tracks make it go faster on roads | Tracks let it drive up walls
exercise: Why must you never go onto a building site, even when it is empty? | Machines, holes and falling things are dangerous and drivers can't see you | The workers want to keep their secrets | You might get your shoes dirty | The site is only closed to make the job look harder
exercise: What stops a tower crane from tipping over when it lifts something heavy? | Heavy concrete weights on its short arm balance the load | The building next to it holds it up with strong steel ropes | Its cab is very heavy at the top | It only ever lifts very light things
exercise: How does a tower crane grow taller as the building rises? | Pieces are added to its mast | It stretches like elastic as it is pulled | A bigger crane is built on top of it | It climbs up the building on its own legs
exercise: How does a tower crane driver usually get to the cab? | By climbing a long ladder inside the mast | By being lifted up on the hook every morning | By helicopter, dropped off at the start of work | By a long slide from the roof of the next building
exercise: Why does a mobile crane put its legs out before lifting? | So it does not tip over | So it can walk to the next job | To lift itself higher like a stool | To warn people to stay away
exercise: Why does the drum of a cement mixer lorry keep spinning? | So the concrete keeps mixing and does not set | So the driver can see it is working | To keep the concrete cool in summer, so it doesn't melt on the way | To make the lorry go faster
exercise: How does concrete harden? | By a chemical reaction with water | By drying out in the sun like mud | By freezing overnight | By being squashed by a road roller
exercise: What does a bulldozer use to push earth and rubble? | A big blade at the front | A spinning brush at the back | A bucket on a long bendy arm | A hose of very strong water
exercise: What were caterpillar tracks used on before tanks? | Farm tractors | Racing bicycles | Huge sailing ships | Early aeroplanes
exercise: What does a dumper truck do with its load? | Tips its bin up to empty it | Sucks it out with a pipe | Spins it round like a mixer | Lifts it with a crane arm
exercise: Why do some road rollers shake? | To pack the ground and asphalt more tightly | To shake the dust off the driver | To scare birds off the road before the hot asphalt goes down | Because their engines are broken
exercise: What does a pile driver do? | Drives long posts deep into the ground to hold up buildings | Piles up bricks into tall stacks | Drives lorries round the building site | Digs holes for swimming pools
exercise: What keeps a worker safe on a cherry picker platform? | A harness clipped to the platform | A big net hung under the street | A parachute strapped on their back | Holding on tight to the lamppost
exercise: Why are building workers' clothes so bright? | Hi-vis clothes make them easy to see | To match the colour of the cranes | Bright colours keep them warm | So they can find each other at lunch
exercise: Count them: 3 dumper trucks with 6 wheels each. How many wheels? | 18 | 9 | 12 | 24
test: «the site's empty on Sunday, let's go play on the sand piles» What is right? | Never go in: building sites are dangerous even when empty | Fine, there are no machines on Sundays, so there's nothing that can hurt you | Fine, if you stay on the sand | Fine, if one of you keeps watch
test: «concrete's just mud that dries, innit» What is right? | No, it sets by a chemical reaction with water | Yes, it dries in the sun like mud | Yes, so rain makes it go soft and runny again later | No, it sets because it freezes
test: In a cartoon, a crane lifts a whole house and does not tip, with nothing on its short arm. What is wrong? | Without counterweights on the short arm, the crane would tip over | Nothing, cranes are always strong enough | Cranes never lift anything heavy | The house would make the crane taller
test: What would happen if a mixer lorry got stuck in traffic and its drum stopped turning for hours? | The concrete could set hard inside the drum | Nothing, concrete never sets while it is inside a lorry | The concrete would turn back into sand | The drum would start turning on its own
test: «the crane's swinging a load over us, cool, let's stand right under it» What is right? | Never stand under a load: it could fall | It's safe, cranes never drop anything, the hooks lock shut | It's fine, if you wear a cap | Only dangerous at night
test: «why's the digger on tracks, wheels'd be faster, they're thick» What is the smart answer? | Tracks spread the weight so the digger does not sink in mud | Tracks are only for show | Wheels would break the digger | Tracks make the digger lighter
test: It is very windy and the tower crane stands still all day. Why? | Cranes stop in high winds to stay safe | The driver could not find the ladder up | Cranes only work in the rain when it's cooler | The crane's battery ran out in the night
test: Why do tall buildings need piles under them? | To carry their weight down to firm ground | To keep water out of the basement | To stop worms getting in | To give the tower crane something firm to stand on while it works
test: «a monster truck at the mine had tyres taller than my dad» Could that be true? | Yes, the biggest mining dump trucks have tyres taller than an adult | No, no tyre is taller than a bike wheel | No, mine trucks have no tyres, only tracks | Only in films
test: «how do they fix the street lights, climb the pole?» What do they usually use? | A cherry picker that lifts them on a platform | They throw a rope over the top | They take the whole lamppost down each time and put it back up after | They use a tall kitchen ladder
test: Count them: a mobile crane puts out 4 legs, and there are 3 cranes on the site. How many legs? | 12 | 7 | 4 | 16
exam: Identify the principle by which the arm of an excavator exerts considerable force upon a slight movement of the control lever. | Hydraulics, whereby a pump forces oil, which is incompressible, into pistons | The arm is connected to the lever by a rope and pulley | The bucket is rendered buoyant by the admission of air | Magnets attract the soil into the bucket
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the hardening of concrete. | Concrete hardens by means of a chemical reaction with water | Concrete hardens solely by evaporation of its water in the heat of the sun | Concrete hardens exclusively by freezing | Concrete hardens only when compressed by a roller
exam: Ascertain the function of the concrete blocks mounted upon the short arm of a tower crane. | They counterbalance the load so that the crane does not overturn | They furnish the crane driver with a place to rest | They are spare materials awaiting use in the construction of the building | They render the crane more conspicuous to aircraft
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct required of a child in respect of a building site? | A child shall not enter a building site, even when it appears to be unattended | A child may enter a building site provided that the machines are stationary | A child may enter a building site on Sundays | A child may enter a building site if wearing a hard hat
